//! The default interactive layout **and keymap**. It is seeded to
//! `~/.config/eidolon/ui.rn` on first launch and that copy is the one the
//! harness compiles: edit it there, and `:reload_ui` reads it again. A
//! copy that does not compile falls back to this (with the error shown).
//! An upgrade carries the copy forward while it is untouched and leaves
//! it alone once edited; the shipped version is then in
//! `~/.config/eidolon/system/ui.rn` to diff against, with `prelude.rn` —
//! the builders this calls, compiled in front of every `ui.rn` — beside it.
//!
//! ## The entry points
//!
//! `view(s)` gets a snapshot each frame: model, persona (the vault note
//! this session is wearing, empty when none), session, cwd, working,
//! elapsed_ms, context_tokens, turn_out (this turn's output so far, a
//! streamed estimate until the settle), life (the pulse's strip of Life,
//! one row of braille), usage_in, usage_out, spend (a dollar string,
//! empty until a priced turn has settled), tools_run, last_tool,
//! dialog, following, detail, copied, queued, transcript_len, tick,
//! `mode` (with `mode_colour`, `mode_label`, `mode_sigil` and
//! `mode_kind` beside it), `pending` (the chord typed so far) and
//! `empty` (is the prompt blank).
//!
//! `modes()` and `theme()` are the two tables below, read once at load
//! along with `keymap()`, and `commands()` is a third this script does
//! not need: a table of commands of your own — `#{ "shout":
//! custom_arg("shout", "TEXT", "send TEXT in capitals") }` with `pub fn
//! shout(s, arg) { send(`SHOUT ${arg}`) }` beside it — which are then
//! bound, typed on the `:` line, completed and printed by `:help`
//! exactly as the built-ins are. A command's function
//! answers with the same actions `on_submit` does, plus `insert(text)`
//! and `info(text)`. `custom_fill` says where the argument's values come
//! from as well as what it is called, and the `:` line then completes it
//! — see the prelude. `:reload_ui` reads all four tables again — so
//! changing a colour, a badge, a binding or a command is a save and a
//! command.
//!
//! `detail` is how much tool traffic the transcript is drawing:
//! "folded" (a settled run of calls is one summary line), "calls" (every
//! call, capped output) or "full" (every call, all of it). `space o`
//! cycles it.
//!
//! The status line's left half is borrowed for a few seconds by a
//! *notice* — what the last keystroke came to: "no matches", "yanked 2
//! blocks", "no such command" — drawn by Rust over whatever `left(s)`
//! returned, so a layout of your own shows it without asking. It is
//! never on the transcript, which is for the conversation. `:messages`
//! pages through every notice said so far. A page (`:help`, `:context`,
//! `:tree`, `:messages`, `:usage`) is a dialog the way the pickers are:
//! the `page` table below is its keys and `modes()` has its badge.
//!
//! `mode` is "normal", "insert" or "select" — the prompt's own — or one
//! of the three **one-line modes**, "command", "search" and
//! "dispatch", which are not editing modes at all: each is a second,
//! shorter buffer (the *minibuffer*) written along the prompt's bottom
//! border, with the message you were composing still in the box above
//! it and still being searched. All three are reported as modes because
//! they are modes to the hands, and the frame says which one has the
//! keyboard.
//!
//! That the message stays put is the point. The `:` line used to *be*
//! the prompt, dispatch was a mode the prompt was put into, and the
//! search line stood in the prompt's screen slot — so reaching for any
//! of the three took a half-written message off the screen, and
//! searching the session for the very thing you were in the middle of
//! saying meant losing what you had said of it.
//!
//! `menu()` fills with completion while the `:` line is up, so
//! the same popup that shows an open chord shows what could come next:
//! the matching commands before the space, and — past it — what could go
//! in *that command's argument*, from the source its row names (a path,
//! a model key, a mode, a record id, a staged image). `tab` walks the
//! list and `up`/`down` do too; a name that takes an argument completes
//! with the space already on the end. An argument that is prose gets its
//! shape said (`[TEXT]`) and nothing offered.
//!
//! `mode_colour`, `mode_label` and `mode_sigil` are that mode's row of
//! `modes()`, handed back rather than looked up here again: the frame
//! round the prompt, the title on it and the badge below all read one
//! answer, so they cannot disagree. Every mode names itself on its
//! frame, and each one-line mode wears its own character at the head of
//! its line, in the same colour: `:` command, `/` search, `?` search
//! back, `!` dispatch. All four are the `sigil` on that mode's row —
//! drawn, never stored, so the buffer holds the command, the pattern or
//! the utterance alone. Drop the sigil from a row and none is drawn;
//! `?` is the one exception, standing in for `/` when the search is
//! going backwards, because a direction is not a mode.
//!
//! `s.dispatch` names the surface the dispatch line is aimed at ("" when
//! none is configured) — see "Dispatches" below.
//!
//! `keymap()` is read **once**, at startup: a table of mode → key →
//! command. It is pure data, because the state a modal keymap needs (the
//! open chord, the count, the mode) lives in Rust — a Rune VM is built per
//! call and could not remember that you pressed `g` a moment ago. There is
//! no `on_key` any more; this table replaced it.
//!
//! `on_submit(s, text)` still decides what a submitted line *means*.
//!
//! ## The keys
//!
//! Helix, with one deliberate departure, forced by this being a chat
//! prompt rather than a file:
//!
//!  * **`j`/`k` scroll the transcript, not the prompt.** There are two
//!    surfaces and one keyboard: vertical motion inside a three-line
//!    prompt is rare, and scrolling back through a session is not. The
//!    prompt's own vertical motion is on `up`/`down`. Consequently
//!    `gg`/`ge` are the *prompt's* ends, Helix-faithfully, and the
//!    transcript's top is `gt`.
//!  * **`gw` and `gW` are the letter tags**, over the two things on the
//!    screen worth pointing at: `gw` labels every word in the visible
//!    conversation and in the draft, `gW` labels every block. Type the
//!    label — one letter, or two on a busy screen — and you are there.
//!    A word in the prompt is a motion (select mode extends to it); a
//!    word in the transcript has no cursor to be, so landing on one
//!    selects it and yanks it, which is what one wants a word out of a
//!    transcript for; a block lands the trace cursor.
//!
//! **Normal is the resting mode**, as in Helix. Insert was tried first —
//! a chat prompt is mostly typing, so opening in insert saves an `i` per
//! message — but it costs the whole keyboard: `:` typed a colon instead of
//! opening the command line, and the leader needed an `esc` in front of it
//! that nothing on screen suggested. A modal prompt that is never in the
//! mode where keys mean things is not modal. So `i` starts a message and
//! `esc` comes back, and a blank prompt is the harness's keyboard.
//!
//! Sending, though, is not a reason to leave insert: the prompt comes
//! back empty in the mode the message was written in, so a run of
//! messages costs one `i` and not one per message. The dispatch line
//! keeps its line up for the same reason, so a run of utterances costs
//! one `!`; the `:` line and the search are done with when they are
//! answered, and come down.
//!
//! **The readline keys are bound wherever text is typed** — insert, the
//! three one-line modes, and a dialog's field. `C-a`/`C-e` are the ends
//! of the line, `C-b`/`C-f` and `A-b`/`A-f` are the two step sizes,
//! `C-u`/`C-k`/`C-w`/`A-d` are the four kills and `C-y` puts back what
//! the last one took. They are not a second modal vocabulary: they are
//! what every unix text field has done for forty years, and the hand
//! that reaches for `C-a` mid-message is not in Helix at the time.
//! Normal and select keep the Helix keyboard exactly — `^`, `$`, `w`,
//! `d` already say all of it in one keystroke there.
//!
//! There is one control binding, and it is not a vocabulary — `space` is
//! the leader, and the popup that opens under it says what it can become.
//! `C-t` is the exception because the operator's own Helix config already
//! made it one: `T` is the prefix that opens another window there, so
//! `till_prev_char` moved out of its way, and a harness that put it
//! somewhere else would fork the hands rather than follow them.
//!
//! ## Another window
//!
//! `T` opens one, where helix and yazi both already open one: `T T` a
//! terminal, `T R` the file manager, `T H` the editor, `T E` another
//! eidolon. All four open **here** — and *here* is the session's working
//! directory in normal mode and, in trace mode, the file the call under
//! the cursor touched. That last part is the half only this tool can
//! offer: it is the one of the three that knows what was just written.
//!
//! Which programs those are is `[terminal]` in `config.toml`, and with no
//! table the harness works it out on the first press — `$TERM` for the
//! emulator it is running inside, `$EDITOR` for the editor. Nothing is
//! resolved at startup, because the launch budget is ten milliseconds and
//! a session that never presses `T` should not pay for a `PATH` scan.
//!
//! ## The vocabulary
//!
//! A binding names a command from the harness's one registry, and so does
//! the `:` line — `space m` and `:model` are the same command reached two
//! ways, and `space p` is a second key onto that line. A name the
//! registry does not know is reported at startup rather than silently
//! doing nothing, so this table cannot invent a command.
//! `ui::commands()` hands the table to a script that wants to build a
//! menu or a help page of its own.
//!
//! `space p` used to open a *palette* — the registry as a filterable
//! list. It is gone. The `:` line completes the same table with the same
//! ranking, and it shows you the command it resolved to before you run
//! it, where a list ran whatever was under the highlight; it also
//! completes the argument, which a list of names cannot. What the
//! palette was better at was browsing, and `:help` is better at that
//! still: the whole vocabulary, grouped, on a page you can scroll, with
//! the live keymap above it.
//!
//! There is no `/` **command prefix**. A submitted line beginning with a
//! slash is ordinary prompt text and goes to the model; the command line
//! is `:` and only `:`. The `/` *key* is a different thing and is bound,
//! in normal mode, to the transcript's search — exactly as in Helix, and
//! with no ambiguity, because a message is typed in insert mode, where
//! `/` is a character like any other.
//!
//! ## Dispatches
//!
//! `!` (or `space d d`) opens **dispatch mode**: the line is read by a
//! local classifier and becomes one typed tool call, with no model turn.
//! `ret` resolves and runs it, `tab` changes surface, `esc` comes back.
//! Typing there is insert's, to the character — the difference is only
//! what `on_submit` does with the line, which is why the table below is
//! insert's with one key added, and why `on_submit` branches on the mode
//! before it looks at the text.
//!
//! ## Finding things
//!
//! `/` and `?` search the transcript, incrementally: the matches light up
//! as you type and the view follows the nearest one. `ret` keeps it (`n`
//! and `N` then walk the matches), `esc` drops it and puts the view back.
//! `*` searches for whatever the prompt has selected.
//!
//! `tab`, while the search line is up, is the quick half: every match on
//! screen takes a letter, and typing that letter goes there. `gn` does
//! the same after the line has come down, and `gw` is Helix's `gw` — the
//! same tags over the prompt's own words.
//!
//! A match can be somewhere that is not drawn — inside folded tool
//! traffic, or below the cap on an output. Those are still matches, and
//! the line standing in for them says so: `Wrote 3 files · 2 matches`,
//! in the highlight's own colour. `S-tab` on the search line opens it
//! without leaving the search, and the search line only offers that
//! while there is something to open. Both that key and `tab` live on
//! the search line and nowhere else — see "where a binding lives".
//!
//! Rune rules worth knowing before editing: no `let mut` (everything is
//! mutable), `()` cannot be interpolated, and match an `Option` by variant
//! rather than comparing to unit.

pub fn view(s) {
    // A session no one has spoken in opens on the start screen; `:help`
    // has the rest. Not "an empty transcript": model, persona, dispatch
    // and cwd may all change before the first message, and the screen's
    // rows read them live — the changes interact with the screen, they
    // do not dismiss it.
    let body = if s.fresh { start(s) } else { transcript() };
    column([
        fixed(1, empty()),
        fill(body),
        // The prompt grows with what is in it, from one text row to
        // ten. A message is not a line — a paste, a stack trace, a
        // paragraph written with `S-ret` — and a box pinned at one row
        // hides all but the row the caret is on.
        grow(3, 12, prompt(titled(s))),
        fixed(1, status(left(s), right(s))),
        menu(),
        dialog(),
    ])
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------- the start screen
//
// The agent, the name, and enough to begin. One `text` node per row, so
// a narrow terminal clips the art rather than wrapping it. The keys are
// written by hand: a map of the keymap would be the help page again.

fn start(s) {
    // [wordmark, sprite, ink, tint]. The wordmark is padded to one width, so
    // the sprite column stays straight; both colours ramp down the six rows.
    let art = [
        ["  ▄▄▄▄▄▄▄               ▄▄             ", "      ", "#2f6fd0", ""],
        [" █▀██▀▀▀        █▄       ██            ", "  ██  ", "#2f81d0", "#ffd43b"],
        ["   ██     ▀▀    ██       ██       ▄    ", "██████", "#2f92d0", "#ffbe36"],
        ["   ████   ██ ▄████ ▄███▄ ██ ▄███▄ ████▄", "  ██  ", "#2fa4d0", "#ffa830"],
        ["   ██     ██ ██ ██ ██ ██ ██ ██ ██ ██ ██", "██  ██", "#2fb5d0", "#ff922b"],
        ["   ▀█████▄██▄█▀███▄▀███▀▄██▄▀███▀▄██ ▀█", "      ", "#2fc7d0", ""],
    ];
    let rows = [fixed(1, empty())];
    for r in art {
        rows.push(fixed(1, text([bold(` ${r[0]}`, r[2]), bold(`      ${r[1]}`, r[3])])));
    }
    let persona = if s.persona != "" { s.persona } else { "none" };
    let surface = if s.dispatch != "" { s.dispatch } else { "none" };
    let line = |k, v| fixed(1, text([dim(`  ${k}  `), styled(v, "gray")]));
    let keys = |k, v| fixed(1, text([dim("  "), bold(k, "cyan"), styled(`  ${v}`, "gray")]));
    rows.push(fixed(1, empty()));
    rows.push(line("model   ", s.model));
    rows.push(line("persona ", persona));
    rows.push(line("dispatch", surface));
    rows.push(line("cwd     ", s.cwd));
    rows.push(fixed(1, empty()));
    rows.push(keys("i", "type a message · ret sends · S-ret is a newline · esc back to normal"));
    rows.push(keys(":", "a command · space p too · tab completes"));
    rows.push(keys("/", "search the transcript · ! a dispatch line"));
    rows.push(keys("space", "leader: m model · c context · n new · r resume · u usage · o tool detail · q quit"));
    rows.push(keys("?", "space ? is the key reference · :help lists every command"));
    column(rows)
}

/// The mode badge. Colour carries it faster than the letters do.
fn badge(s) {
    // Both halves come from the mode's row in `modes()` below, by way of
    // the snapshot: the same answer the prompt's border and its title are
    // drawn from, so the badge cannot say one thing while the frame round
    // the text says another. Dispatch being the loud one is decided in
    // that table, once.
    on(` ${s.mode_label} `, s.mode_colour)
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------- the modes
//
// Every mode the harness has, as a row: what the badge says, what colour
// the frame wears, and — for the ones the prompt can be in — what *kind*
// of mode it is, which is the one thing the buffer needs to know.
//
//  * **normal**, **insert** and **select** are the three kinds, and the
//    three names Helix's verbs reach (`i`, `v`, `esc`), so their kind is
//    fixed; restyle them freely.
//  * **command**, **search** and **dispatch** have a look but no kind.
//    They are the **one-line modes**: what is being typed there is not
//    the message, it is a command, a pattern or an utterance, and it is
//    written on the prompt's bottom border with the message still in
//    the box above it. So they are modes to the hands without being
//    modes on the buffer, and the badge and frame say so like any
//    other. Three colours and not one: two one-line modes wearing the
//    same colour is exactly the confusion the colour is for, and
//    dispatch is loud on purpose — `ret` there runs a tool.
//
// To add a mode of your own: a row here, a table for it in `keymap()`
// (with a way out — `esc` to `normal_mode`), a binding or a `:enter_mode
// NAME` to get in, and a branch in `on_submit` if `ret` should mean
// something new there. `enter_mode` with no name is a picker over this
// table.

pub fn modes() {
    #{
        "normal":   mode("normal", "NOR", "blue"),
        "insert":   mode("insert", "INS", "green"),
        "select":   mode("select", "SEL", "magenta"),
        "dispatch": sigil(style("DISP", "red"), "!"),
        // Trace mode is normal-kinded — nothing is typed there — and
        // wears a colour nothing else does. Cyan would have read well
        // and is the command line's; orange is free, and a mode that
        // takes the whole keyboard should not borrow another's badge.
        "trace":    mode("normal", "TRACE", "#ff8800"),
        "command":  sigil(style("CMD", "cyan"), ":"),
        // One sigil, two directions: a backward search wears `?` for
        // the `/` this row names, because which way `n` goes is not a
        // mode. Drop the sigil and neither is drawn.
        "search":   sigil(style("FIND", "yellow"), "/"),
        // The dialogs, by kind. They own the keyboard while they are up
        // and take their keys from the tables of the same names below.
        "confirm":  style("ASK?", "yellow"),
        "choose":   style("CHOOSE", "yellow"),
        "pick":     style("PICK", "yellow"),
        "ask":      style("ASK", "yellow"),
        // A page of text (`:help`, `:context`, `:tree`, `:messages`):
        // read, scrolled and closed, on the `page` table below.
        "page":     style("PAGE", "yellow"),
        "vault":    style("VAULT", "yellow"),
    }
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- labels
//
// What tools are called on the transcript, as against their wire names —
// `wait_for` draws as "Waiting", `choices_user`'s question box is titled
// "Options". Cosmetic by construction: dispatch, the log, search and the
// fold summary all read the wire name, so a label changes what a call
// says and never what it is. A tool not named here draws under its own
// name, which is right for most calls.

pub fn labels() {
    #{
        "wait_for": "Waiting",
        "choices_user": "Options",
    }
}

// A kind core does not watch here is a warning at load; a kind this
// table leaves out draws under its own name.

pub fn wait_tags() {
    #{
        "bg": "bg",
        "peer": "peer",
        "file": "file",
        "log": "log",
        "vault": "vault",
    }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------- the theme
//
// Every colour Rust paints on its own account, by role. What this script
// draws itself — the status line, a panel — takes its colour inline; this
// is for the things placed rather than drawn: the search highlight, the
// letter tags, the popup, a dialog, the tool traffic, the model's
// markdown. The modes' colours are on their rows above, not here. A
// colour is a name or `#rrggbb`; a theme need only name what it changes.

pub fn theme() {
    #{
        hit: "yellow",        // a search match, and the count on a fold that hides one
        tag: "magenta",       // a letter tag, and the offer to open what hides a match
        menu: "yellow",       // the which-key popup and command completion
        dialog: "yellow",     // an approval, question or picker
        ok: "green",          // the allow key on an approval
        error: "red",         // a failed tool, a search with no match, the deny key
        user: "green",        // your own messages
        peer: "lightgreen",   // a message from another session on this project
        tool: "magenta",      // the arrows and names on tool traffic
        faint: "dim",         // notes, captions, folded output: the terminal's own dimming
        text: "default",      // secondary text that is not faint: the terminal's foreground
        sent: "lightcyan",    // what a call sent: the command, the path, the pattern
        gutter: "dim",        // a code block's line numbers: a darker shade of the foreground
        comment: "darkgray",  // a comment inside a code block
        string: "green",      // a quoted string inside a code block
        number: "magenta",    // a number inside a code block
        keyword: "blue",      // a language's reserved word inside a code block
        block: "#262336",     // behind a fenced block — a light theme wants its own
        added: "#1c3a28",     // behind a line a diff puts in
        removed: "#3a1f26",   // behind a line a diff takes out
        cursor: "yellow",     // the streaming caret
        focus: "reversed",    // the block the trace cursor is on: the terminal's inversion, softened
        code: "cyan",         // inline code and fenced blocks
        heading: "yellow",    // level one and two headings
        link: "blue",         // link labels
    }
}

fn left(s) {
    // No session name here: sixteen digits of epoch, and the notices need the room.
    // While a turn runs the pulse stands where the model name does —
    // one line either way, and a row of its own was a row the
    // transcript gave up on every turn.
    let out = [badge(s), dim(" "), bold(s.model, "cyan")];
    // Who is speaking, next to what is speaking. Empty means nobody is
    // pinned, which is the ordinary case and draws nothing — and it is
    // drawn here rather than further down because a persona is not an
    // event in the session, it is a standing fact about every turn in it,
    // the same kind of thing the model key beside it is.
    if s.persona != "" {
        out.push(dim(" · "));
        out.push(bold(s.persona, "magenta"));
    }
    // The gate is answering for you. Everything else on this line is a
    // fact about the session; this is a fact about what is no longer
    // happening to it, so it is loud and it is early. It is also the only
    // thing that says so — a yolo yes draws nothing in the transcript,
    // deliberately, because a line per waived call in the mode built to
    // stop interrupting is the interruption again.
    if s.yolo {
        out.push(bold(" · YOLO", "red"));
    }
    if s.mode == "dispatch" {
        out.push(bold(` · ${s.dispatch}`, "red"));
    }
    if s.tracing {
        // Where in the transcript the cursor is standing, what it is
        // standing on, and whether there is a fold here to open. A
        // cursor you cannot locate is a cursor you scroll to find.
        out.push(bold(` · ${s.trace_at}/${s.trace_blocks}`, "#ff8800"));
        out.push(dim(` ${s.trace_kind}`));
        if s.trace_filter != "" {
            // The word the walk is narrowed to, in the cursor's own
            // colour: a filtered 3/7 should say why it is seven and
            // not seventy.
            out.push(bold(` · ${s.trace_filter}`, "#ff8800"));
        }
        if s.trace_open {
            out.push(dim(" · l opens"));
        }
        if s.trace_extend {
            out.push(bold(` · EXT ${s.trace_span}`, "magenta"));
        }
    }
    if s.pending != "" {
        out.push(bold(` · ${s.pending}`, "yellow"));
    }
    if s.queued > 0 {
        out.push(dim(` · ${s.queued} queued`));
    }
    if !s.following {
        out.push(dim(" · ↑ scrolled"));
    }
    if s.copied {
        out.push(dim(" · copied"));
    }
    // Folded is the resting step and says so on the transcript itself
    // ("Ran 3 shell commands"); the other two are the unusual state, so
    // they are the ones worth a word here.
    if s.detail != "folded" {
        out.push(dim(` · ${s.detail}`));
    }
    return out;
}

/// The pulse: a strip of Conway's Life above the prompt while the model
/// runs — `s.life`, stepped by the harness on its tick, with a shape
/// thrown in by every tool call. The word turns over every eight calls
/// and the folded thinking line wears the same one. Then the clock, and
/// what went in and has come out so far.
/// The prompt frame's title: the mode's name, and the agent after it.
/// At rest the agent is two cells standing still; while a turn runs it
/// is the strip those cells grow into, from the same column, so the
/// figure starts moving from where it already was.
fn titled(s) {
    let out = [bold(` ${s.mode} `, s.mode_colour)];
    if s.working {
        for span in agent(s) {
            out.push(span);
        }
    } else {
        out.push(bold(ui::agent_icon(), "#ffd43b"));
    }
    return out;
}

fn agent(s) {
    let secs = s.elapsed_ms / 1000;
    let clock = if secs >= 60 { `${secs / 60}m${secs % 60}s` } else { `${secs}.${(s.elapsed_ms / 100) % 10}s` };
    let sent = if s.context_tokens >= 0 { `⇡ ${ui::k(s.context_tokens)} ` } else { "" };
    let gauge = ` ${clock} · ${sent}⇣ ${ui::k(s.turn_out)}`;
    // The strip is shaded along its length: `#ff922b` at the left, `#ffd43b`
    // at the right where the word picks it up.
    let hex = |n| format!("{:02x}", n);
    let width = s.life.chars().count();
    let out = [];
    for (x, cell) in s.life.chars().enumerate() {
        out.push(bold(`${cell}`, `#ff${hex(0x92 + 0x42 * x / width)}${hex(0x2b + 0x10 * x / width)}`));
    }
    out.push(bold(`  ${ui::cogitation(s.tools_run)}`, "yellow"));
    out.push(dim(gauge));
    out
}

fn right(s) {
    // `142.3k/200.0k (71%)`, and a bare count where the model declares no
    // window. This was roman numerals over a thousand — `ctx XII k` —
    // which was charming at a glance and useless at the only moment the
    // gauge is read: a long session, where the question is how much room
    // is left and the answer arrived as something to decode, against no
    // denominator to decode it against.
    // `spend` is already a dollar string, and empty until a turn on a
    // model whose definition declares rates has settled — so nothing is
    // drawn for a session whose cost the harness cannot know.
    let spend = if s.spend != "" { ` · ${s.spend}` } else { "" };
    [dim(`ctx ${ui::context(s.context_tokens, s.context_limit)} · in ${ui::k(s.usage_in)} out ${ui::k(s.usage_out)}${spend} · tools ${s.tools_run} `)]
}

pub fn on_submit(s, text) {
    // Dispatch mode answers first, and answers on the *mode* rather than on
    // anything in the text: the prompt was aimed at a classifier before a
    // word of this line was typed, so every character of it — a leading
    // colon included — is part of the utterance.
    if s.mode == "dispatch" {
        return dispatch(text);
    }
    // `parse_command` returns a real Option: match the variant, never
    // compare against unit (see the vault's Rune gotchas note). It takes
    // `:name` and nothing else — `/` is reserved, so a line beginning with
    // a slash is ordinary text and goes to the model like any other.
    //
    // The name it hands back is resolved against the one command registry,
    // so `:` reaches the prompt's own commands (`:goto_buffer_end`) as
    // readily as the harness's (`:compact`). `ui::commands()` is that
    // table, if a script wants to build its own menu from it.
    match ui::parse_command(text.clone()) {
        Some(cmd) => command(cmd.name, cmd.arg),
        None => send(text),
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- the keymap
//
// Fitted to the operator's own Helix and yazi keymaps (~/.config/nix), so
// the three tools agree about what a key means. What was taken from there:
//
//  * **Shift-arrows, in every mode.** `S-up`/`S-down` page, `S-left` goes
//    to the first non-blank and `S-right` past the end of the line — and
//    the Helix config clones all four into insert and select rather than
//    leaving them to normal mode. That is not just taste: `foot` binds
//    `Page_Up`/`Page_Down` to its own scrollback, so the bare page keys
//    never reach a program running in it. They stay bound here anyway, for
//    a terminal that does pass them through.
//  * **`^` is the hard start of the line and `$` the position past its
//    end**, which is Helix's `goto_line_start` / `goto_line_end_newline`
//    and the reverse of vim's `^`. First-non-blank is `S-left` and `gs`.
//  * **`space q` quits and `space w` sends**, after `:q` and `:w`.
//  * **`space r` resumes and `space n` starts a session** — `r` for
//    resume, and `n` beside it for the other half of the same question.
//    Both are on the leader rather than at top level: moving between
//    sessions is deliberate, and `n` at top level is the search's.
//  * **`K` shows more**, where Helix has it on `hover`: here the nearest
//    thing under the cursor is the transcript, so it puts a cursor on it
//    — trace mode. It used to step the *global* detail knob, which
//    answered "show me this" by opening every fold in the session; the
//    knob is still on `space o`, where a preference belongs.
//  * **A `desc` on every binding**, as in the yazi keymap — which is what
//    fills the popup, so nothing has to be remembered to be found.
//  * **`T T` and `T R` open another window**, which is the one chord the
//    helix and yazi keymaps *share* — the same two keys spawn a terminal
//    and a yazi from either of them, so they have stopped being two
//    programs' bindings and become the hands'. A third tool in that
//    rotation that answered `T` with `till_prev_char` would be the only
//    place the rotation broke, so it answers with the window instead, and
//    `till_prev_char` follows the same key to `C-t` that it took in the
//    Helix config for the same reason. `T H` and `T E` complete the set
//    in the direction the other two cannot go: back to the editor, and to
//    a second harness.
//
// One thing in that config has no counterpart here: the `C-`/`C-S-arrow`
// view jumps want splits, which this has none of.

// ## Where a binding lives
//
// **A key is bound in the mode where it means something, and nowhere
// else.** That is the whole point of a modal keymap: the popup under
// `space` is a promise about what these keys do *now*, and an entry that
// needs state the mode cannot see turns the promise into a guess.
//
// The search line is the case that forces the rule. It borrows the
// keyboard in front of this table — it is not one of the modes below —
// so its keys (`tab` to tag the matches, `S-tab` to open whatever hides
// one, `esc`, `ret`) live in `search_key` on the Rust side. What follows
// is that `goto_hit` and `reveal_match` are *not* bound here, however
// useful they sound: both are meaningless without a live search, so
// binding them in normal mode would advertise an action that usually
// cannot run. They stay reachable by name, because `:` reaches the whole
// registry by design.
//
// `n`, `N` and `*` are the apparent exception and are not one: those are
// Helix's own normal-mode search keys, they are what an operator's hands
// already expect there, and with no search they say so rather than doing
// nothing.

pub fn keymap() {
    // Select mode is normal mode with the motions extending. Only the two
    // ways *out* differ, so override those rather than repeat the table.
    // (`select` is a Rune keyword; the binding cannot be called that.)
    let extend = normal_keys();
    extend.insert("esc", cmd("normal_mode", "normal mode"));
    extend.insert("v", cmd("normal_mode", "normal mode"));

    #{
        "normal": normal_keys(),
        "select": extend,
        "insert": insert_keys(),
        "command": command_keys(),
        "dispatch": dispatch_keys(),
        "trace": trace_keys(),
        // The search line and the dialogs: not modes on the buffer, but
        // modes to the hands, so they are tables here rather than keys
        // hard-wired in Rust — which is what lets the popup promise what
        // `tab` does on the search line, and lets you rebind `n`.
        "search": search_keys(),
        "confirm": #{
            "y": cmd("submit", "allow"),
            "ret": cmd("submit", "allow"),
            "n": cmd("dialog_deny", "deny"),
            "esc": cmd("cancel", "dismiss"),
        },
        // The question dialog is a picker of a different shape: the
        // options are paragraphs, `j`/`k` move, a digit picks its option
        // outright, and `ret` takes the one under the arrow.
        "choose": #{
            "up": cmd("move_line_up", "previous"),
            "down": cmd("move_line_down", "next"),
            "k": cmd("move_line_up", "previous"),
            "j": cmd("move_line_down", "next"),
            "ret": cmd("submit", "choose"),
            "esc": cmd("cancel", "dismiss"),
        },
        "pick": pick_keys(),
        "ask": ask_keys(),
        "page": page_keys(),
        "vault": vault_keys(),
    }
}

/// A page of text: the transcript's own scroll keys, `y` to copy the
/// whole of it, `q` or `esc` to close. Vault pages also take tab/shift-tab
/// and enter for links, backspace for the note history. Nothing here types.
fn page_keys() {
    return #{
        "tab": cmd("link_next", "next vault link"),
        "S-tab": cmd("link_prev", "previous vault link"),
        "ret": cmd("link_open", "open vault link"),
        "backspace": cmd("link_back", "previous vault note"),
        "j": cmd("scroll_line_down", "down a line"),
        "k": cmd("scroll_line_up", "up a line"),
        "down": cmd("scroll_line_down", "down a line"),
        "up": cmd("scroll_line_up", "up a line"),
        "A-down": cmd("scroll_half_down", "half a page down"),
        "A-up": cmd("scroll_half_up", "half a page up"),
        "S-down": cmd("scroll_page_down", "a page down"),
        "S-up": cmd("scroll_page_up", "a page up"),
        "pagedown": cmd("scroll_page_down", "a page down"),
        "pageup": cmd("scroll_page_up", "a page up"),
        "g": group("goto", #{
            "g": cmd("transcript_top", "the top"),
            "e": cmd("transcript_bottom", "the end"),
        }),
        "y": cmd("yank", "copy the page"),
        "q": cmd("cancel", "close"),
        "esc": cmd("cancel", "close"),
    };
}

/// The reader borrows normal's editor/leader vocabulary; only navigation differs.
fn vault_keys() {
    let m = normal_keys();
    for (key, value) in page_keys() { m.insert(key, value); }
    m.insert("q", cmd("link_close", "return to the conversation"));
    m.insert("g", group("goto", #{
        "w": cmd("goto_word", "tag visible words and links"),
        "g": cmd("transcript_top", "the top"),
        "e": cmd("transcript_bottom", "the end"),
    }));
    return m;
}

/// The plain picker: type to filter, arrows to walk, enter to choose.
fn pick_keys() {
    return line_keys(#{
        "up": cmd("move_line_up", "previous"),
        "down": cmd("move_line_down", "next"),
        "ret": cmd("submit", "pick"),
        "esc": cmd("cancel", "dismiss"),
    });
}

/// A single masked field: type, `ret` to store it, `esc` to cancel. No
/// list to walk, so it takes only the readline keys `pick` also takes.
fn ask_keys() {
    return line_keys(#{
        "ret": cmd("submit", "store the key"),
        "esc": cmd("cancel", "dismiss"),
    });
}

/// The search line. `tab` and `S-tab` live here and nowhere else — see
/// "where a binding lives" — and everything else is insert's, because
/// the pattern is a minibuffer: an ordinary buffer on the prompt's
/// bottom border, which selects, yanks and undoes like the prompt does.
fn search_keys() {
    let m = insert_keys();
    // The editor chord is the prompt's — see insert_keys — and a search
    // pattern is not the prompt.
    m.remove("C-x");
    m.insert("esc", cmd("cancel", "drop the search"));
    m.insert("ret", cmd("submit", "keep the search"));
    m.insert("tab", cmd("goto_hit", "tag the matches"));
    m.insert("S-tab", cmd("reveal_match", "open what hides the match"));
    // A pattern is one line, so `up`/`down` have nothing to walk in it —
    // they step through the patterns typed before, which is the search
    // history and is what the keys mean in every other search field.
    m.insert("up", cmd("history_prev", "previous pattern"));
    m.insert("down", cmd("history_next", "next pattern"));
    return m;
}

/// A one-line field's motions, shared by the pickers and the dialogs
/// that have one. The search line and the `:` line no longer come
/// through here: they are minibuffers, which is to say ordinary
/// [`Buffer`]s, so they take insert's whole table.
fn line_keys(m) {
    m.insert("left", cmd("move_char_left", "left"));
    m.insert("right", cmd("move_char_right", "right"));
    m.insert("home", cmd("goto_line_start", "line start"));
    m.insert("end", cmd("goto_line_end_newline", "line end"));
    m.insert("backspace", cmd("delete_char_backward", "delete back"));
    return readline(m);
}

/// **The readline keys**, on every surface text is typed into.
///
/// The harness is modal and Helix-shaped, and that is not in tension
/// with this: `C-a` and `C-e` are not a second modal vocabulary, they
/// are what *every* unix text field has done since the eighties, and the
/// hands that reach for them are not in Helix at the time — they are in
/// a shell, a browser bar, a readline prompt. A modal editor that made
/// them mean nothing would be asking the operator to unlearn the one set
/// of bindings they cannot avoid elsewhere.
///
/// So they are bound where text is typed, and nowhere else. Normal and
/// select mode keep the Helix keyboard exactly as it was: `^`, `$`, `w`,
/// `b`, `d`, `c` already say all of this in one keystroke, and a control
/// key there would be a second way to spell a motion rather than a
/// second place to reach one.
///
/// The four kills fill the register, so `C-y` puts back what the last
/// one took — readline's kill ring, in the one slot the buffer has.
fn readline(m) {
    m.insert("C-a", cmd("goto_line_start", "line start"));
    m.insert("C-e", cmd("goto_line_end_newline", "line end"));
    m.insert("C-b", cmd("move_char_left", "left"));
    m.insert("C-f", cmd("move_char_right", "right"));
    m.insert("A-b", cmd("move_prev_word_start", "word back"));
    m.insert("A-f", cmd("move_next_word_end", "word forward"));
    // The arrows with ctrl, which is what every text field on the machine
    // does with them. They are *spelled* like GUI word-jumps and *meant*
    // like readline's word motion — the same two commands `A-b`/`A-f` run
    // — because a hand that is already on the arrows is not reaching for
    // a different convention, only for the same one without leaving home
    // row. `C-left` in a terminal that cannot tell it from anything else
    // never arrives; that is the terminal's loss, not a broken key.
    m.insert("C-left", cmd("move_prev_word_start", "word back"));
    m.insert("C-right", cmd("move_next_word_end", "word forward"));
    // And the ends of the whole buffer, which is what ctrl says on home
    // and end everywhere else. A prompt is usually one line, but `S-ret`
    // exists, and a multi-line draft is exactly the thing you want the
    // top or bottom of.
    m.insert("C-home", cmd("goto_buffer_start", "buffer start"));
    m.insert("C-end", cmd("goto_buffer_end", "buffer end"));
    m.insert("C-h", cmd("delete_char_backward", "delete back"));
    m.insert("C-d", cmd("delete_char_forward", "delete forward"));
    m.insert("C-u", cmd("delete_to_line_start", "kill to line start"));
    m.insert("C-k", cmd("delete_to_line_end", "kill to line end"));
    m.insert("C-w", cmd("delete_word_backward", "kill word back"));
    m.insert("A-d", cmd("delete_word_forward", "kill word forward"));
    // The kills at word size, on the keys a browser and a text field use
    // for it: `C-backspace` and `C-del` behind and in front, and
    // `A-backspace` because that is the same gesture said the other way.
    // A terminal without the kitty protocol folds `C-backspace` into
    // `C-h` — one character, which is what that terminal can say — so
    // nothing here can shadow anything.
    m.insert("C-backspace", cmd("delete_word_backward", "kill word back"));
    m.insert("C-del", cmd("delete_word_forward", "kill word forward"));
    m.insert("A-backspace", cmd("delete_word_backward", "kill word back"));
    m.insert("C-y", cmd("paste_before", "put back what was killed"));
    // Readline's undo. The slash is what the key says under the kitty
    // protocol, which the loop asks for; the underscore is what readline
    // itself names, and both arrive as themselves — in a legacy terminal
    // they are one byte, `C-7`, and undo is the least of what that
    // terminal has lost.
    m.insert("C-/", cmd("undo", "undo"));
    m.insert("C-_", cmd("undo", "undo"));
    // Readline's transpose: drag the character behind the caret over the
    // one at it. Normal mode keeps `C-t` for the till it displaced — the
    // two never share a table, so the letter means one thing per mode.
    m.insert("C-t", cmd("transpose_chars", "transpose"));
    return m;
}

/// Dispatch mode types exactly like insert; where the line *goes* is
/// decided in `on_submit`, not here. So the table is insert's, with
/// three keys changed: `tab` picks the surface, `esc` takes the line
/// down (it is a minibuffer now, not the prompt in another mode), and
/// `up`/`down` walk the utterances typed before rather than the
/// messages sent.
fn dispatch_keys() {
    let m = insert_keys();
    // The editor chord is the prompt's — see insert_keys — and an
    // utterance is not the prompt.
    m.remove("C-x");
    m.insert("tab", cmd("dispatch_surface", "next surface"));
    m.insert("esc", cmd("cancel", "drop the utterance"));
    m.insert("up", cmd("history_prev", "previous utterance"));
    m.insert("down", cmd("history_next", "next utterance"));
    return m;
}

/// The command line is the prompt in insert with a `:` in it, so its
/// table is insert's — plus completion, which means something here and
/// nowhere else (see "where a binding lives"). A script without this
/// table gets insert's for the `:` line, so leaving it out costs only
/// `tab`.
fn command_keys() {
    let m = insert_keys();
    // The editor chord is the prompt's — see insert_keys — and a command
    // is not the prompt.
    m.remove("C-x");
    m.insert("tab", cmd("complete_next", "next completion"));
    m.insert("S-tab", cmd("complete_prev", "previous completion"));
    m.insert("esc", cmd("cancel", "drop the line"));
    // The arrows walk the popup here. A `:` line is one line, so the
    // vertical motion they carry in insert has nothing to move through —
    // and the list hanging over the prompt is the one thing on screen
    // that up and down obviously mean.
    m.insert("down", cmd("complete_next", "next completion"));
    m.insert("up", cmd("complete_prev", "previous completion"));
    m.insert("C-n", cmd("complete_next", "next completion"));
    m.insert("C-p", cmd("complete_prev", "previous completion"));
    // The commands typed before, since the arrows are spoken for. The
    // `:` line has a history of its own now — it used to leave every
    // command in the *message* history, because it was the prompt.
    m.insert("A-p", cmd("history_prev", "previous command"));
    m.insert("A-n", cmd("history_next", "next command"));
    return m;
}

/// The four shift-arrows, which the Helix config clones into every mode.
/// Anything bound here is bound in all three.
fn shared(m) {
    m.insert("S-up", cmd("scroll_page_up", "page up"));
    m.insert("S-down", cmd("scroll_page_down", "page down"));
    m.insert("S-left", cmd("goto_first_nonwhitespace", "first non-blank"));
    m.insert("S-right", cmd("goto_line_end_newline", "past line end"));
    // Bound for terminals that do not eat them; foot does.
    m.insert("pageup", cmd("scroll_page_up", "page up"));
    m.insert("pagedown", cmd("scroll_page_down", "page down"));
    m.insert("A-up", cmd("scroll_half_up", "half page up"));
    m.insert("A-down", cmd("scroll_half_down", "half page down"));
    return m;
}

fn normal_keys() {
    return shared(#{
        // Leaving normal mode.
        "i": cmd("insert_mode", "insert"),
        "a": cmd("append_mode", "append"),
        "I": cmd("insert_at_line_start", "insert at line start"),
        "A": cmd("insert_at_line_end", "append at line end"),
        "o": cmd("open_below", "open line below"),
        "O": cmd("open_above", "open line above"),
        "v": cmd("select_mode", "select mode"),
        ":": cmd("command_line", "command line"),
        // `!` for a dispatch, after the shell's own "run this". It is a
        // top-level key and not only a leader chord because it is a *mode*,
        // and the other three ways into a mode (`i`, `a`, `v`) are one key.
        "!": cmd("dispatch_mode", "dispatch mode"),
        "ret": cmd("submit", "send"),
        // Esc cancels a turn in flight; with nothing running it just drops
        // the selection. It is the only panic button there is.
        "esc": cmd("cancel", "cancel turn"),

        // Motion. Every one of these extends instead of reselecting when
        // the mode is select — same command, different mode.
        "h": cmd("move_char_left", "left"),
        "l": cmd("move_char_right", "right"),
        "left": cmd("move_char_left", "left"),
        "right": cmd("move_char_right", "right"),
        "up": cmd("move_line_up", "line up"),
        "down": cmd("move_line_down", "line down"),
        "w": cmd("move_next_word_start", "next word"),
        "b": cmd("move_prev_word_start", "prev word"),
        "e": cmd("move_next_word_end", "word end"),
        "W": cmd("move_next_long_word_start", "next WORD"),
        "B": cmd("move_prev_long_word_start", "prev WORD"),
        "E": cmd("move_next_long_word_end", "WORD end"),
        // `^` is column zero and `$` the slot past the last char, matching
        // the operator's Helix overrides rather than Helix's own defaults.
        "^": cmd("goto_line_start", "line start"),
        "$": cmd("goto_line_end_newline", "past line end"),
        "0": cmd("goto_line_start", "line start"),
        "home": cmd("goto_line_start", "line start"),
        "end": cmd("goto_line_end_newline", "past line end"),
        "f": cmd("find_next_char", "find char"),
        "t": cmd("till_next_char", "till char"),
        "F": cmd("find_prev_char", "find char back"),
        // `T` is the window prefix below, exactly as in the operator's
        // helix and yazi keymaps, so Helix's `T` moves to the key Helix's
        // own config moved it to. It is the harness's only control
        // binding and is not a precedent: it is one displaced motion
        // following its own config, not a second vocabulary.
        "C-t": cmd("till_prev_char", "till char back"),
        "T": window(),

        // The transcript's search, on Helix's keys. `/` is safe here for
        // the same reason `:` is: this is normal mode, where letters are
        // commands. A message beginning with a slash is typed in insert,
        // where it is text and stays text.
        "/": cmd("search", "search the transcript"),
        "?": cmd("rsearch", "search backwards"),
        "n": cmd("search_next", "next match"),
        "N": cmd("search_prev", "previous match"),
        "*": cmd("search_selection", "search for the selection"),

        // Selection.
        "x": cmd("select_line", "select line"),
        "X": cmd("extend_to_line_bounds", "take whole lines"),
        "A-x": cmd("shrink_to_line_bounds", "snap back to whole lines"),
        "%": cmd("select_all", "select all"),
        ";": cmd("collapse_selection", "collapse"),
        "A-;": cmd("flip_selections", "flip anchor and cursor"),

        // Change. Each acts on the selection a motion just made.
        "d": cmd("delete_selection", "delete"),
        "A-d": cmd("delete_selection_noyank", "delete, register left alone"),
        "c": cmd("change_selection", "change"),
        "A-c": cmd("change_selection_noyank", "change, register left alone"),
        // Helix master dropped these two from its defaults, but the hands
        // kept them — the till the muscle memory asks for when a line's
        // tail has to go.
        "D": cmd("delete_to_line_end", "delete to line end"),
        "C": cmd("change_to_line_end", "change to line end"),
        "y": cmd("yank", "yank"),
        "p": cmd("paste_after", "paste after"),
        "P": cmd("paste_before", "paste before"),
        "R": cmd("replace_with_yanked", "replace with yanked"),
        "r": cmd("replace", "replace char"),
        "~": cmd("switch_case", "switch case"),
        "`": cmd("switch_to_lowercase", "to lowercase"),
        "A-`": cmd("switch_to_uppercase", "to uppercase"),
        "J": cmd("join_selections", "join lines"),
        ">": cmd("indent", "indent"),
        "<": cmd("unindent", "unindent"),
        "C-a": cmd("increment", "number up"),
        "C-x": cmd("decrement", "number down"),
        ".": cmd("repeat_last_insert", "the last typing, again"),
        "A-.": cmd("repeat_last_motion", "the last find, again"),
        "u": cmd("undo", "undo"),
        "U": cmd("redo", "redo"),

        // The transcript. j/k here rather than in the prompt: see above.
        // `K` is Helix's hover key — more detail about what is in front of
        // you — so it steps the tool traffic up: folded runs, every call,
        // then uncapped output, and back.
        "j": cmd("scroll_line_down", "down a line"),
        "k": cmd("scroll_line_up", "up a line"),
        // Helix has `K` on hover — more about the thing in front of you
        // — and here the thing in front of you is the transcript. It
        // used to step the *global* detail knob, which answered "show
        // me this" by opening every fold in the session; now it puts a
        // cursor on the transcript instead. The knob is still on
        // `space o`, where a preference belongs.
        "K": cmd("trace_mode", "trace the transcript"),

        "g": group("goto", #{
            "h": cmd("goto_line_start", "line start"),
            "l": cmd("goto_line_end_newline", "past line end"),
            "s": cmd("goto_first_nonwhitespace", "first non-blank"),
            "g": cmd("goto_buffer_start", "prompt start"),
            "e": cmd("goto_buffer_end", "prompt end"),
            "t": cmd("transcript_top", "transcript top"),
            "b": cmd("transcript_bottom", "follow the tail"),
            // Helix's `gw`, on what Helix aims it at: the text on the
            // screen. `W` is the same gesture at the bigger unit —
            // lowercase the smaller thing and uppercase the larger one,
            // as `w`/`W` and `e`/`E` already are — because a screenful
            // is a couple of hundred words and about a dozen blocks, and
            // one set of labels cannot be both.
            //
            // Both belong here and their search-side twin (`goto_hit`)
            // does not: there is always something on screen to tag,
            // whereas tagging *matches* presupposes a search. See "where
            // a binding lives" above.
            "w": cmd("goto_word", "tag words"),
            "W": cmd("goto_block", "tag blocks"),
            // With a count, the column; bare, the line start `0` already
            // names — the count is what makes the chord worth its keys.
            "|": cmd("goto_column", "column N"),
        }),

        // Helix's `G`: line N, or the last line when no count was typed.
        "G": cmd("goto_line", "line N, or the last"),

        // Match mode: brackets, the objects they and the quotes make,
        // and the surround verbs that work on them. `miw`, `ma(`, `ms"`,
        // `mr(`, `md"` — the sub-keys say which.
        "m": group("match", #{
            "m": cmd("match_bracket", "matching bracket"),
            "i": cmd("select_object_inner", "inside"),
            "a": cmd("select_object_around", "around"),
            "s": cmd("surround_add", "surround with CHAR"),
            "r": cmd("surround_replace", "swap pair for pair"),
            "d": cmd("surround_delete", "take the pair off"),
        }),

        // The unimpaired pair: paragraphs are the break a draft has that
        // is bigger than a line, and a blank line is room made without
        // going to insert.
        "[": group("prev", #{
            "p": cmd("goto_prev_paragraph", "previous paragraph"),
            "space": cmd("add_newline_above", "add a line above"),
        }),
        "]": group("next", #{
            "p": cmd("goto_next_paragraph", "next paragraph"),
            "space": cmd("add_newline_below", "add a line below"),
        }),

        "space": leader(),
    });
}

/// **Another window**, on the chord the operator's other two tools share.
///
/// `T T` is a terminal and `T R` a file manager in both helix and yazi,
/// which is what makes them worth copying: two programs binding the same
/// two keys to the same two things is no longer either program's keymap,
/// it is the hands'. This is the third program, so the set closes: `T E`
/// reaches a harness from all three, `T H` reaches the editor from the
/// two that are not it, and `T R` and `T T` mean here what they already
/// meant there.
///
/// Every one of them opens **here**, and the word does the work: in
/// normal mode *here* is the session's working directory, and in trace
/// mode it is wherever the cursor is standing — the file the call under
/// it read, wrote or edited. So `K`, `j` to the write, `T H` is the
/// whole gesture for *open what it just changed*, and the same three
/// keys with `R` hands that file to yazi instead.
///
/// A function rather than a literal for the reason [`leader`] is one:
/// trace mode wants exactly this table, and it is in trace mode that the
/// cursor makes it interesting.
///
/// Select mode gets it too, by inheriting normal's table — which is one
/// step further than the Helix config went, where `T` is rebound in
/// normal alone and select keeps `extend_till_prev_char`. Following that
/// exactly would mean `T` meaning two different things a keystroke
/// apart, which is worse than the small loss: `C-t` is bound in select as
/// well, and motions extend there, so the displaced till is still one
/// chord away and still extends.
fn window() {
    return group("window", #{
        "T": cmd("terminal", "a terminal here"),
        "R": cmd("files", "the file manager here"),
        "H": cmd("editor", "the editor here"),
        "E": cmd("harness", "another eidolon here"),
    });
}

/// The leader. Everything that used to be a control key is here, and the
/// popup lists it, so nothing is unfindable. `w` sends and `q` quits,
/// after `:w` and `:q`. These are not a second kind of binding: `m`
/// names the same `model` command `:model` runs, and `p` opens the
/// palette, which is the whole list of them.
///
/// A function rather than a literal because trace mode wants the same
/// one: the leader is the harness's vocabulary, and a mode that made
/// `space q` mean nothing would be a mode you have to leave to quit.
fn leader() {
    return group("eidolon", #{
        "w": cmd("submit", "send"),
        "m": cmd("model", "model picker"),
        "L": cmd("login", "provider login"),
        // The fork point is a cursor, not a dialog: bare, `fork` moves the
        // head to the record the trace cursor stands on (`K`, `j`, here),
        // because the rows a picker would list are the rows `K` walks.
        "f": cmd("fork", "fork at the cursor"),
        "t": cmd("tree", "session tree"),
        "r": cmd("sessions", "resume a session"),
        "n": cmd("new_session", "new session"),
        // A group for the reason `d` and `a` are groups: context is a
        // small vocabulary and the popup should teach all of it from one
        // keystroke. Compaction keeps a path one key deeper than it had
        // rather than moving somewhere unguessable.
        "c": group("context", #{
            "c": cmd("compact", "compact"),
            "i": cmd("context", "inspect the context"),
            "n": cmd("note", "session note"),
            "p": cmd("pin", "pin a file"),
            "f": cmd("fresh", "fresh eyes"),
        }),
        // A group rather than a leaf, because the submodes land here:
        // one key per surface, once there is more than one.
        //
        // `s` is here as well as on `tab` inside the mode, and that is
        // not a violation of "where a binding lives": the surface is
        // *sticky* UI state, not something the mode holds, so aiming
        // from normal mode and then pressing `!` is a real sequence
        // and the popup's promise is kept. It is `n`/`N`'s case, not
        // `reveal_match`'s — with one bundle configured it says so
        // rather than doing nothing.
        "d": group("dispatch", #{
            "d": cmd("dispatch_mode", "dispatch mode"),
            "s": cmd("dispatch_surface", "next surface"),
        }),
        // Images do not need a leader sub-vocabulary of their own: the
        // common case is `C-v`, which attaches what is on the clipboard.
        // The file and clear commands stay on the `:` line for the rarer
        // cases where a literal path or an explicit unstage is wanted.
        "o": cmd("cycle_detail", "more tool detail"),
        "K": cmd("trace_mode", "trace the transcript"),
        // `yolo` is deliberately **unbound**. It was `space y`, one key
        // deep, on the reasoning that a switch you reach for after the
        // fourth question in a row is one you reach for in a hurry — but
        // `y` is yank, in normal mode and in trace mode, and one letter
        // meaning both "copy this" and "stop asking me about anything"
        // is the one overload worth refusing. It is `:yolo` now, and a
        // binding of your own if you want one.
        // The `:` line, on the leader as well as on its own key. It is
        // the whole vocabulary, filtered and completed, and it shows you
        // the command it resolved to before you run it — which is what
        // the command palette that used to be here could not do.
        "p": cmd("command_line", "command line"),
        // The status line's four numbers, unfolded: which turn cost
        // what, and why.
        "u": cmd("usage", "usage: what the session has spent"),
        "?": cmd("help", "help"),
        "q": cmd("quit", "quit"),
    });
}

/// **Trace mode**: a cursor on the transcript rather than on the prompt.
///
/// Helix's own shape, pointed at the other surface. `j`/`k` walk the
/// blocks, `l`/`h` go a level down and a level up, `v` extends and `y`
/// yanks — the same four verbs the prompt has, so nothing has to be
/// learned twice. What is different is what a block *is*: a message, a
/// note, or a whole **cluster** — everything the model did between one
/// piece of prose and the next, drawn as one line and opened with `l`.
///
/// The search keys are here because the search is how you get to a block
/// you cannot see, and `*` searches for what the transcript has selected
/// rather than what the prompt has. `n` moves the cursor and opens
/// nothing: the fold it lands on already says how many matches it is
/// hiding, and `l` is one key.
///
/// The leader is here too. A mode you have to leave in order to quit, or
/// to change model, is a mode you resent.
///
/// The cursor is also a *fork point*: `space f` moves the head to the record
/// under it. That is what the tree picker used to list, and the rows are the
/// ones this mode already walks.
fn trace_keys() {
    return shared(#{
        // Out. `K` is the key that got you in, so it is the key that
        // gets you out; `esc` and `i` are the two ways out of every
        // other mode and mean the same here.
        "esc": cmd("normal_mode", "leave trace"),
        "K": cmd("trace_mode", "leave trace"),
        "!": cmd("dispatch_mode", "dispatch mode"),
        "backspace": cmd("link_back", "previous vault note"),
        "q": cmd("link_close", "return to the conversation"),
        "i": cmd("insert_mode", "write a message"),
        ":": cmd("command_line", "command line"),

        // The cursor.
        "j": cmd("trace_next", "next block"),
        "k": cmd("trace_prev", "previous block"),
        "down": cmd("trace_next", "next block"),
        "up": cmd("trace_prev", "previous block"),
        "l": cmd("trace_open", "open this block"),
        "right": cmd("trace_open", "open this block"),
        "h": cmd("trace_close", "close the fold"),
        "left": cmd("trace_close", "close the fold"),
        "ret": cmd("link_open", "open vault link"),
        "tab": cmd("link_next", "next vault link"),
        "S-tab": cmd("link_prev", "previous vault link"),

        // Selection, exactly as in the prompt: `v` extends, `y` takes
        // what is selected. `y` fills the prompt's register as well as
        // the clipboard, so `p` pastes a tool's output back at the model.
        "v": cmd("trace_extend", "extend the selection"),
        "y": cmd("trace_yank", "yank the selection"),
        "Q": cmd("trace_quote", "quote vault selection by address"),
        // Strike what the cursor covers from the context. A toggle: a
        // selection that is already struck comes back.
        "x": cmd("trace_strike", "strike from the context"),

        // Finding things. The search line takes the keyboard while it is
        // up and hands the cursor whatever it lands on.
        "/": cmd("search", "search the transcript"),
        "?": cmd("rsearch", "search backwards"),
        "n": cmd("search_next", "next match"),
        "N": cmd("search_prev", "previous match"),
        "*": cmd("search_selection", "search for the selection"),

        "g": group("goto", #{
            "w": cmd("goto_word", "tag visible words and links"),
            "g": cmd("trace_first", "first block"),
            "e": cmd("trace_last", "last block"),
            "t": cmd("trace_first", "first block"),
            "b": cmd("trace_last", "last block"),
        }),
        // Helix's fold chord, on the folds this transcript has.
        "z": group("folds", #{
            "o": cmd("trace_open_all", "open every fold"),
            "c": cmd("trace_close_all", "close every fold"),
            "z": cmd("trace_toggle", "open or close this one"),
        }),
        // The window chord means more here than it does in normal mode,
        // and is the reason it is a function: `T H` on a call opens the
        // editor on the file that call touched, and `T R` hands the same
        // file to the file manager. In normal mode both fall back to the
        // session's directory, which is what they do in helix and yazi.
        "T": window(),
        "o": cmd("cycle_detail", "more tool detail"),
        "space": leader(),
    });
}

fn insert_keys() {
    return readline(shared(#{
        // A pasted image should be the easy chord and plain text the
        // explicit one, matching Claude Code and the common terminal
        // convention: `C-v` tries the clipboard as an image, `C-S-v`
        // forces a literal text paste.
        "C-v": cmd("attach_clipboard", "attach what you copied"),
        "C-S-v": cmd("paste_before", "paste text"),
        // **The draft, in your editor** — readline's `C-x C-e`, one chord
        // deep the way bash and zsh spell it. It is a prefix rather than a
        // leaf because `C-x` is the prefix readline itself keeps for
        // "special, deliberate things", and because a whole control key
        // spent on one command is more than the keyboard should pay. The
        // minibuffers derive this table and take the chord back off: the
        // editor takes the prompt, and the prompt only.
        "C-x": group("editor", #{
            "e": cmd("edit_prompt", "the draft in your editor"),
        }),
        "esc": cmd("normal_mode", "normal mode"),
        "ret": cmd("submit", "send"),
        "S-ret": cmd("insert_newline", "newline"),
        "A-ret": cmd("insert_newline", "newline"),
        // On the prompt this deletes and no more. On a one-line buffer — a
        // `:` line, a pattern, an utterance — a backspace with nothing
        // left in front of it takes the line down instead, because
        // emptied is how such a line is usually left. `esc` still does it
        // from anywhere on it, and a script that binds some other verb
        // here loses the shortcut and nothing else.
        "backspace": cmd("delete_char_backward", "delete back"),
        "del": cmd("delete_char_forward", "delete forward"),
        "left": cmd("move_char_left", "left"),
        "right": cmd("move_char_right", "right"),
        "home": cmd("goto_line_start", "line start"),
        "end": cmd("goto_line_end_newline", "past line end"),
        // Up/down walk the history here; the prompt's own line motion is
        // in normal mode, where a multi-line draft is edited anyway.
        "up": cmd("history_prev", "previous message"),
        "down": cmd("history_next", "next message"),
        "C-p": cmd("history_prev", "previous message"),
        "C-n": cmd("history_next", "next message"),
        // Mid-burst undo boundary: `C-s` closes the run, so what is typed
        // next undoes on its own. Readline leaves the key to flow control,
        // which a raw terminal has long since stopped meaning.
        "C-s": cmd("commit_undo_checkpoint", "undo checkpoint"),
    }));
}
